        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"The town hired me to remove names from benches.\\n\\nNot the plaques. Those stayed. I sanded away the names people scratched into the wood.\\n\\n“Only the names,” my supervisor said. “Leave the hearts.”\\n\\nIt was easy work until I found my brother’s.\\n\\nHe had been missing for twenty years.\\n\\nThe letters were fresh, pale curls of wood still caught inside the R.\\n\\nI called my supervisor.\\n\\n“Common name,” she said.\\n\\n“His handwriting.”\\n\\n“Sand it.”\\n\\nInstead, I sat down.\\n\\nSomeone had carved a heart beside his name. Inside it was tomorrow’s date.\\n\\nI returned before sunrise with coffee and the pocketknife he’d given me when I was twelve.\\n\\nAt seven, an old woman approached the bench.\\n\\n“You’re in his place,” she said.\\n\\n“My brother’s?”\\n\\n“My husband’s.”\\n\\nShe showed me a photograph. My brother stood beside her, gray-haired, holding a fish.\\n\\n“He never liked fishing,” I said.\\n\\n“He learned.”\\n\\nShe touched the carved heart.\\n\\n“We had forty-three years. Please don’t make him start again.”\\n\\nAcross the park, my supervisor was unlocking the equipment shed.\\n\\n“How do I bring him home?” I asked.\\n\\nThe woman looked at my knife.\\n\\n“You give the bench another name.”\\n\\nI thought of our mother, who still left the porch light on.\\n\\nThen I opened the blade.\\n\\nThe old woman sat beside me while I worked.\\n\\nI carved slowly. There was a splinter under my thumbnail, and my hand was shaking.\\n\\nWhen I finished, she took my hand.\\n\\nI knew her then.\\n\\n“Did you catch anything?” she asked.\\n\\n“Not today,” I said.\\n\\nAcross the park, a young man stood watching us. He looked so much like my brother that I almost called out.\"\n}",
